{"conjecture": "Software systems accumulate implicit invariants—unstated relationships between components that are never encoded in types, tests, or interfaces—so each locally correct change subtly widens the gap between what the code says and what the system actually depends on, increasing the probability that the next change violates a constraint nobody knows exists. This creates an asymmetry: every merge adds hidden coupling, but no review or test can remove it, because the coupling is invisible by definition. The system degrades not because any change is wrong, but because the space of safe changes shrinks faster than developers can discover its boundaries."}
